## Usage

```bash
pulumi stack init dev
pulumi config set --secret dbPassword '...'   # encrypted via ConfigSecret
pulumi up                                      # dev: db.t3.micro, single-AZ, no replication

pulumi stack init prod
pulumi config set --secret dbPassword '...'
pulumi up                                      # prod: db.t3.medium, multi-AZ, replication
```

## Acceptance criteria mapping

| Criterion | Where |
|---|---|
| Bucket versioned + SSE, no public access | `versioning`, `serverSideEncryptionConfiguration` (KMS + bucket key), `BucketPublicAccessBlock` |
| RDS deletion protection, backups ≥ 7d, private | `deletionProtection: true`, `backupRetentionPeriod: 7`, `publiclyAccessible: false` |
| Secret never plaintext | `cfg.requireSecret` + `pulumi.secret()` on connection string |
| Least privilege, no wildcards | Explicit IAM actions scoped to bucket/db ARNs |
| Dev/prod isolation | Separate stacks/configs — `pulumi up` on `prod` never touches `dev` state |
| Replication (prod only) | `bucketReplicaRegion` set only in `prod.yaml` |

**Notes:** `ACCOUNT` in the OIDC trust policy should be replaced with the EKS cluster OIDC provider output once the EKS stack exists (`cluster.core.oidcProvider.arn`). The ComponentResource gives a clean tree in `pulumi stack` and a reusable unit for other apps.
